// ============================================================
// Supplement front matter — "The argument, briefly."
// The digital supplement carries minimal apparatus: this two-page
// digest stands in for the main volume's full introduction.
// ============================================================

#import "../lib/theme.typ": *
#import "../lib/components.typ": *

#pagebreak(weak: true)

#text(font: sans, size: 8pt, tracking: 1.6pt, fill: gold, upper("About this supplement"))
#v(6pt)
#text(font: serif, size: 24pt, fill: navy, "The argument, briefly")
#v(10pt)

#text(font: sans, size: 9.5pt, fill: text-dark)[
  This volume is the digital supplement to *Capability Matters: A
  Casebook*. The printed main volume carries a selected set of
  cases — five to eleven per part — chosen to span the corpus's
  domains, failure modes, evidence tiers, and signature lessons.
  This supplement carries every other case at full depth, in the
  same seven-part order, under the same editorial rules. Numbering
  is global across both books: a gap in the printed sequence is a
  case that lives here, and every case citation (by number or by
  slug) resolves identically in either volume.
]

#v(8pt)

#text(font: sans, size: 9.5pt, fill: text-dark)[
  The casebook's claim is that *capability* — the interface between
  what a system requires of its human operators and the impact the
  system must deliver — is a designable, measurable, engineerable
  property of complex systems, and that treating it as an
  afterthought is the common mechanism behind failures that are
  usually filed under separate headings. The cases are organized
  topically in seven parts: Healthcare & Patient Safety; Education,
  Training & the Learning Workforce; Aviation & Aerospace; Defense &
  National Security; Industry, Energy & Enterprise Systems; Disaster
  Prevention & Recovery (read along the pre/post lifecycle of the
  event rather than a domain); and Algorithms, Governance & Public
  Systems. Each part splits into *What Fails* and *What Works — and
  the Frontier*.
]

#v(8pt)

#text(font: sans, size: 9.5pt, fill: text-dark)[
  Two analytic threads run across every part. The first is the
  six-mode failure taxonomy carried on each case header — T Training
  Gap · D Designed Out · N Normalization of Deviance · H Human-System
  Interface · G Governance & Trust · K Knowledge & Institutional
  Memory. The second is the *paired intervention*: every success case
  couples a mechanism to an authorization — a checklist to a nurse
  empowered to stop the line, a reporting form to immunity, a
  measurement instrument to a governance body that acts on it.
  Neither half alone moves the curve.
]

#v(8pt)

#text(font: sans, size: 9.5pt, fill: text-dark)[
  Every case carries three anchors (canonical LENS competency,
  induced sub-competency, and CLO), a scale tier, and an
  evidence-source tag. Weaker-evidence cases render an evidence-tier
  flag under the title with standing "future validation ongoing"
  language; cases touching the editors' own institutions or work
  render a conflict-of-interest disclosure in the same position.
  Load-bearing causal hedges are never smoothed. For the full
  intellectual framing, the reading pathways, and the program
  context, see the main volume's introduction; for the program
  documents of record, the LENS Companion; for the consolidated
  per-case references appendix with retrieval lines and the
  verification record, the Validation & Audit tracker.
]

#v(1fr)
#line(length: 32mm, stroke: 0.8pt + gold)
